   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.3",
    "topic_title": "Cellular Energy",
    "stem": "Reaction X joins molecule A to molecule B to form AB. On its own, Reaction X requires an input of energy and does not proceed to any significant extent. In a cell, the reaction occurs as follows: an enzyme first transfers a phosphate group from ATP to A, forming A–P and ADP. A–P then reacts with B, forming AB and releasing inorganic phosphate. Which statement best explains how ATP makes the synthesis of AB possible?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Phosphate transfer from ATP produces a reactive, less stable A–P intermediate, so the energy released by ATP hydrolysis is coupled to the energy-requiring formation of AB and the combined process releases energy overall.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The energy-requiring process is joining A to B. ATP drives it through phosphate transfer: phosphorylating A creates a reactive intermediate, linking the energy-releasing breakdown of ATP to AB formation. Because the coupled steps together release energy, the overall process can proceed. This is energy coupling, not just ATP being energy."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "ATP acts as a catalyst that lowers the activation energy of Reaction X, so A and B combine faster without ATP being changed.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses energy coupling with enzyme catalysis. ATP is consumed and becomes ADP plus phosphate, so it is not a catalyst, and lowering activation energy only speeds a reaction without supplying the energy an energy-requiring reaction needs.Fix: Ask whether the molecule is chemically changed by the end of the process; if it is used up, it is a reactant, not a catalyst."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "All of the energy released from ATP is transferred into the new bond in AB, so no energy is lost as heat during the coupled reaction.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats ATP as a packet of energy moved perfectly into AB, which violates the second law of thermodynamics. In every energy transfer some energy is lost as heat, which is why cells need continual energy input to maintain order.Fix: Check any energy-transfer claim against the second law by asking where some energy escapes as heat."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The formation of AB releases energy, and the cell uses that energy to regenerate ATP from ADP and phosphate.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reverses which process releases energy and which requires it. The stem states that forming AB requires energy input, so AB synthesis cannot be the energy source; it is ATP breakdown that supplies the energy.Fix: Identify from the stem which reaction requires energy, then confirm the proposed energy source is the process that releases energy."
     }
    ]
   },
